### What is the problem?

Given a sorted array that may contain duplicates and a target value, find the first (leftmost) and last (rightmost) index where the target appears. If the target is not present, return [-1, -1]. The solution must run in O(log n) time.

A plain binary search finds some occurrence of the target, but with duplicates it could land anywhere in the run. We need the exact boundaries.

```java
package com.bank.search;

public class FirstLastPosition {

    public static int[] searchRange(int[] amounts, int target) {
        int first = findFirst(amounts, target);
        if (first == -1) return new int[]{-1, -1};
        int last = findLast(amounts, target);
        return new int[]{first, last};
    }

    private static int findFirst(int[] arr, int target) {
        int low = 0, high = arr.length - 1, result = -1;
        while (low <= high) {
            int mid = low + (high - low) / 2;
            if (arr[mid] == target) {
                result = mid;
                high = mid - 1;   // keep searching left
            } else if (arr[mid] < target) {
                low = mid + 1;
            } else {
                high = mid - 1;
            }
        }
        return result;
    }

    private static int findLast(int[] arr, int target) {
        int low = 0, high = arr.length - 1, result = -1;
        while (low <= high) {
            int mid = low + (high - low) / 2;
            if (arr[mid] == target) {
                result = mid;
                low = mid + 1;    // keep searching right
            } else if (arr[mid] < target) {
                low = mid + 1;
            } else {
                high = mid - 1;
            }
        }
        return result;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[] txnAmounts = {100, 200, 200, 200, 300, 400, 500};
        int[] range = searchRange(txnAmounts, 200);
        System.out.println("First: " + range[0] + ", Last: " + range[1]);
        System.out.println("Count: " + (range[1] - range[0] + 1));
    }
}
```

```text
First: 1, Last: 3
Count: 3
```

### Why does the bias work?

By never returning early on a match and always pushing toward the desired edge, the last stored match is guaranteed to be the extreme occurrence. For the first-position search, every time we find the target we move `high` below `mid`, so any earlier occurrence would be found on a later iteration. The loop only ends when no more leftward movement is possible, so the last recorded index is the leftmost.

```java
package com.bank.search;

public class BiasExplained {

    public static int findFirstWithTrace(int[] arr, int target) {
        int low = 0, high = arr.length - 1, result = -1;
        int step = 0;
        while (low <= high) {
            step++;
            int mid = low + (high - low) / 2;
            System.out.println("Step " + step + ": low=" + low
                + " high=" + high + " mid=" + mid + " val=" + arr[mid]);
            if (arr[mid] == target) {
                result = mid;
                System.out.println("  Match! Record " + mid + ", search left");
                high = mid - 1;
            } else if (arr[mid] < target) {
                low = mid + 1;
            } else {
                high = mid - 1;
            }
        }
        return result;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[] arr = {100, 200, 200, 200, 200, 300};
        int first = findFirstWithTrace(arr, 200);
        System.out.println("First position: " + first);
    }
}
```

```text
Step 1: low=0 high=5 mid=2 val=200
  Match! Record 2, search left
Step 2: low=0 high=1 mid=0 val=100
Step 3: low=1 high=1 mid=1 val=200
  Match! Record 1, search left
First position: 1
```

### How is this different from standard binary search?

Standard binary search returns immediately on finding the target. The boundary search does not return early. It records the match and continues toward the desired edge. This single difference is what makes it find the first or last occurrence.

| Behavior | Standard Search | First Position | Last Position |
|---|---|---|---|
| On match | Return immediately | Record, search left | Record, search right |
| Returns | Any occurrence | Leftmost occurrence | Rightmost occurrence |
| Time | O(log n) | O(log n) | O(log n) |

```java
package com.bank.search;

import java.util.Arrays;

public class StandardVsBoundary {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[] arr = {100, 200, 200, 200, 300};
        // Standard: finds index 2 (some occurrence)
        System.out.println("Standard: " + Arrays.binarySearch(arr, 200));
        // Boundary: finds index 1 (first) and 3 (last)
        System.out.println("First: " + findFirst(arr, 200));
        System.out.println("Last:  " + findLast(arr, 200));
    }

    static int findFirst(int[] arr, int t) {
        int lo = 0, hi = arr.length - 1, res = -1;
        while (lo <= hi) {
            int m = lo + (hi - lo) / 2;
            if (arr[m] == t) { res = m; hi = m - 1; }
            else if (arr[m] < t) lo = m + 1;
            else hi = m - 1;
        }
        return res;
    }

    static int findLast(int[] arr, int t) {
        int lo = 0, hi = arr.length - 1, res = -1;
        while (lo <= hi) {
            int m = lo + (hi - lo) / 2;
            if (arr[m] == t) { res = m; lo = m + 1; }
            else if (arr[m] < t) lo = m + 1;
            else hi = m - 1;
        }
        return res;
    }
}
```

```text
Standard: 2
First: 1
Last:  3
```

### How do you count occurrences efficiently?

Once you have the first and last indices, the count is `last - first + 1`. This avoids a separate linear scan through the duplicates.

```java
package com.bank.search;

public class CountOccurrences {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[] amounts = {100, 200, 200, 200, 200, 200, 300, 400};
        int first = 1;  // from findFirst
        int last = 5;   // from findLast
        int count = last - first + 1;
        System.out.println("Amount 200 appears " + count + " times");
    }
}
```

```text
Amount 200 appears 5 times
```

### What are the edge cases?

The important edge cases are: target not present (return [-1, -1]), all elements are the target (first = 0, last = n-1), target at position 0 only, and target at position n-1 only.

```java
package com.bank.search;

import java.util.Arrays;

public class EdgeCases {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Not present:  " + Arrays.toString(searchRange(new int[]{1,2,3}, 5)));
        System.out.println("All same:     " + Arrays.toString(searchRange(new int[]{2,2,2}, 2)));
        System.out.println("First only:   " + Arrays.toString(searchRange(new int[]{2,3,4}, 2)));
        System.out.println("Last only:    " + Arrays.toString(searchRange(new int[]{1,2,3}, 3)));
        System.out.println("Single match: " + Arrays.toString(searchRange(new int[]{5}, 5)));
        System.out.println("Empty:        " + Arrays.toString(searchRange(new int[]{}, 5)));
    }

    static int[] searchRange(int[] a, int t) {
        int f = find(a, t, true);
        if (f == -1) return new int[]{-1, -1};
        return new int[]{f, find(a, t, false)};
    }

    static int find(int[] a, int t, boolean leftBias) {
        int lo = 0, hi = a.length - 1, res = -1;
        while (lo <= hi) {
            int m = lo + (hi - lo) / 2;
            if (a[m] == t) {
                res = m;
                if (leftBias) hi = m - 1; else lo = m + 1;
            } else if (a[m] < t) lo = m + 1;
            else hi = m - 1;
        }
        return res;
    }
}
```

```text
Not present:  [-1, -1]
All same:     [0, 2]
First only:   [0, 0]
Last only:    [2, 2]
Single match: [0, 0]
Empty:        [-1, -1]
```

### What is the time and space complexity?

| Metric | Value |
|---|---|
| Time | O(log n) for each search, O(log n) total |
| Space | O(1) |
| Best case | O(1) if midpoint is the only occurrence |
| Worst case | O(log n) even with all duplicates |

### How is this used in a banking system?

At the National Bank of India, a day's transactions are stored sorted by amount. Many transactions share the same round amount like a popular fifty-thousand-rupee transfer. The audit team needs the first and last index of a given amount to slice out that contiguous block for review. These two searches give exact boundaries in logarithmic time.

```java
package com.bank.search;

import java.math.BigDecimal;

public class BankingAuditSlice {

    public static int findFirst(BigDecimal[] txns, BigDecimal target) {
        int lo = 0, hi = txns.length - 1, res = -1;
        while (lo <= hi) {
            int m = lo + (hi - lo) / 2;
            int cmp = txns[m].compareTo(target);
            if (cmp == 0) { res = m; hi = m - 1; }
            else if (cmp < 0) lo = m + 1;
            else hi = m - 1;
        }
        return res;
    }

    public static int findLast(BigDecimal[] txns, BigDecimal target) {
        int lo = 0, hi = txns.length - 1, res = -1;
        while (lo <= hi) {
            int m = lo + (hi - lo) / 2;
            int cmp = txns[m].compareTo(target);
            if (cmp == 0) { res = m; lo = m + 1; }
            else if (cmp < 0) lo = m + 1;
            else hi = m - 1;
        }
        return res;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        BigDecimal[] txns = {
            new BigDecimal("10000"), new BigDecimal("50000"),
            new BigDecimal("50000"), new BigDecimal("50000"),
            new BigDecimal("75000"), new BigDecimal("100000")
        };
        BigDecimal target = new BigDecimal("50000");
        int first = findFirst(txns, target);
        int last = findLast(txns, target);
        System.out.println("Audit slice: index " + first + " to " + last);
        System.out.println("Matching transactions: " + (last - first + 1));
    }
}
```

```text
Audit slice: index 1 to 3
Matching transactions: 3
```

### What are common interview follow-up questions?

Interviewers often extend this problem:
- Count the number of occurrences (answer: last - first + 1).
- Find the first element greater than the target (upper bound).
- Find the insertion position if the target were to be inserted.
- Apply the same pattern in a rotated sorted array.
